---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import CaseStudyNav from '../../components/CaseStudyNav.astro';
import '../../styles/case-study.css';

import journeyMap1 from '../../assets/images/journey-map-1.png';
import journeyMap2 from '../../assets/images/journey-map-2.png';
import raaJourney from '../../assets/images/raa-journey.png';
import journeyDirectory from '../../assets/images/raa-journey-directory.png';
import raaPersona1 from '../../assets/images/raa-persona.png';
import raaPersona2 from '../../assets/images/detailed-donald.png';
import raaPersona3 from '../../assets/images/quick-quote-quinn.png';
---
<Layout title="Journey Mapping | Andrew Smith" description="Journey mapping as a practice of shared visibility connecting analytics, research, and team knowledge into a single reference.">
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to content</a>
  <div class="case-page">
    <SiteHeader />
    <main id="main-content">

      <section class="case-hero">
        <p class="eyebrow"><a class="breadcrumb-link" href="/portfolio/work">Work</a><span class="breadcrumb-separator">/</span><span>Journey Mapping</span></p>
        <h1 class="display-title">Journey mapping for experience visibility.</h1>
        <p class="hero-text">Most organisations have more data about their customers than they can make sense of. Analytics in one platform, research findings in another, customer feedback somewhere else, and no shared view connecting any of it to the specific moment in the product where the experience is breaking down.</p>
        <p class="hero-text">A well-maintained journey map changes that. It gives product, engineering, service design, and UX a single shared reference so that when decisions get made about what to build next, they are grounded in what is actually known about the experience.</p>
        <p class="hero-subtext">Used across insurance, travel, membership, and identity products.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="meta-strip">
        <div><p class="meta-label">Scope</p><p class="meta-value">Insurance, membership, travel, identity</p></div>
        <div><p class="meta-label">Role</p><p class="meta-value">Senior UX Design Lead</p></div>
        <div><p class="meta-label">Tools</p><p class="meta-value">Journey management platform, Miro, FigJam</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="lead-image-section">
        <Image class="lead-image" src={journeyMap1} alt="Journey explorer showing phases, win rates, drop-offs, emotion curve, and prioritised solutions" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </section>

      <section class="summary-section">
        <p class="section-kicker">Why Journey Management</p>
        <h2>The loop that connects measurement to action.</h2>
        <div class="summary-body">
          <p>The value of journey mapping is not the map itself. It is what happens when it stays connected to real data and real decisions. Benchmarking feeds quantitative metrics into the map at each stage. Research findings tag pain points to specific steps. Analytics gaps surface where the team is making decisions without adequate evidence.</p>
          <p>When product, engineering, and experience design teams arrive at an ideation session with a shared map that already captures what is known, the time goes toward genuine unknowns rather than re-establishing context that already exists. Solutions get prioritised by member value and business value, tagged to the specific stage of the journey where the problem lives, and tracked against the roadmap.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <div class="section-content">
          <p class="section-kicker">Metrics-Connected Maps</p>
          <h2>Live analytics connected to each stage of the experience.</h2>
          <p>The journey explorer connects real analytics to each stage including win rates, drop-off rates, failure rates, and emotion scores. Solutions are prioritised by member value and business value, and the map shows which ones are on the roadmap, in delivery, or on hold.</p>
          <p>This is what a journey map looks like when it is actually used by product and engineering teams alongside experience design. Not a static artefact, but a shared decision-making tool that evolves as the product does.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="section-image">
          <Image class="aside-img" src={journeyMap2} alt="Journey explorer with solution detail panel open showing priority, effort, member value, and business value" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:1200px) 30vw, 480px"/>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <div class="section-content">
          <p class="section-kicker">Research-Grounded Maps</p>
          <h2>Built from synthesis, not from assumption.</h2>
          <p>Before digital journey management tools, maps were built from research synthesis combining interviews, usability sessions, analytics, and team knowledge. The map shows a full member experience from initial awareness through to post-booking, with emotional states, touchpoints, pain points, and opportunities at each stage.</p>
          <p>Maps built this way surface knowledge gaps — the stages where the team is making decisions without adequate research, and where investment in discovery would have the greatest impact. Those gaps feed directly into the discovery process as priority items.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="section-image">
          <Image class="aside-img" src={raaJourney} alt="Journey map showing member stages from research through booking with moods, touchpoints, pain points, and opportunities" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:1200px) 30vw, 480px"/>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <div class="section-content">
          <p class="section-kicker">Journey Map Directory</p>
          <h2>All product areas in one navigable reference.</h2>
          <p>Journey maps across all RAA product areas are maintained in a shared directory covering Road Service, Car Insurance, Home Insurance, Membership, and Solar and Energy. Each map is tagged by lifecycle stage, digital channel availability, and opportunity type, giving product and engineering teams a single reference for where the experience has been mapped and where gaps still exist.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="section-image">
          <Image class="aside-img" src={journeyDirectory} alt="Miro journey map directory showing Road Service, Car Insurance, and Home Insurance product areas" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:1200px) 30vw, 480px"/>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <div class="section-content">
          <p class="section-kicker">Personas</p>
          <h2>Grounded in research, connected to real behaviour data.</h2>
          <p>A persona grounds every design decision in a real human with real motivations and real constraints. These personas were built from research and connected to analytics data showing which member segments show the highest drop-off at each stage of the experience.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <div class="scroll-section">
        <p class="scroll-hint-label">Scroll to see more <span>→</span></p>
        <div class="image-scroll-track-wrap">
          <div class="image-scroll-track">
            <figure class="scroll-fig">
              <Image class="scroll-img" src={raaPersona1} alt="RAA member persona showing background, goals, research behaviour, and device usage" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[420,600]} sizes="420px"/>
              <figcaption>Member persona grounded in research and connected to analytics showing where members drop off</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="scroll-fig">
              <Image class="scroll-img" src={raaPersona3} alt="Quick Quote Quinn persona showing goals, needs, and pain points across the quote journey" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[420,600]} sizes="420px"/>
              <figcaption>Quick Quote Quinn — wants a fast competitive quote without jargon. Drops off if questions are too many or too complex.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="scroll-fig">
              <Image class="scroll-img" src={raaPersona2} alt="Detailed Donald persona showing goals, needs, and pain points for a confident considered purchase" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[420,600]} sizes="420px"/>
              <figcaption>Detailed Donald — confident but cautious. Wants clear cover inclusions and painless member pricing.</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <CaseStudyNav />
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>
